f4999042d25dfc85490e3eaf24b49bce4c2005cefb7ef087b0920ae88e" :answer/id #uuid "f6f8dab9-274c-5180-a5fc-7bc9f2a6838c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Dilation with scale factor $\\frac{1}{17}$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-78337/selection/83351679bef40cdde3e69e55c2c6f08222a317ed82557f1e3ec0abdce5a513f4" :answer/id #uuid "d24c1bda-5422-5419-9ccb-e89a9b284dd7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Reflection across the $y$-axis" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-78337/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "14352b75-10fa-5698-8338-de34f8148a00" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-78337/selection/1d41578cc1b3266c9aa9e886f8b0681f70d37aa14f89f61e1e3e70cee53b7f29" :answer-field/choices ["q-78337/selection/354549e05d30f5acb6c9a5e876a1b220679b1daee3381277145bd16fe273c514" "q-78337/selection/1d41578cc1b3266c9aa9e886f8b0681f70d37aa14f89f61e1e3e70cee53b7f29" "q-78337/selection/114202d7dc0b252bc45dc2c51de228866ad97b456e77b418f70a5b2ccb39f5e5" "q-78337/selection/2638acf4999042d25dfc85490e3eaf24b49bce4c2005cefb7ef087b0920ae88e" "q-78337/selection/83351679bef40cdde3e69e55c2c6f08222a317ed82557f1e3ec0abdce5a513f4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-78337" :question/id #uuid "85f3d75b-9319-5b51-9847-8f5fa211a35d" :question/math-academy-id "q-78337" :question/problem "What linear transformation $T$ is represented by the matrix $T=[\\begin{aligned}17 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 17\\end{aligned}]?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a dilation of scale factor $k$ is represented by the matrix\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}k & 0 \\\\ 0 & k\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nHere, we have $k=17.$ Therefore, the matrix represents a dilation with a scale factor of $17.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-78337/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf99414e-f1cf-528f-adbb-407cf6e82c4c"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-78337"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-78299"] :question/worked-solution "The correct answer is $T=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].$ Indeed, consider the standard basis ${i,j}$ of the Euclidean plane ${R}^{2}$ and the unit square spanned by the vectors, as shown below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052682/assets/9e322e6ed1ddb8f1d094633e931cd179b7eb9d6f47acd7c7d0809f113df91b22.png)\n\nNow, we can find the images of ${i,j}$ under $T$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{′} & =T(i)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=-i \\\\ {j}^{′} & =T(j)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following image:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052682/assets/4993d8586a84e4a40a54edddd8011cd3d516264bfffc7e3c33d27a10edf1e7d7.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5452"] :question/problem "A linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ represents a dilation. Given that ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052682/assets/bd6574ee05b60919de656a889f8532e5a512b381eae63f7a65f251a7afe72738.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052682/assets/be73c2a933a6fb99eaa389774a618848bdadd9cafdf0fcd2f6af126b49b87707.png) what is the standard matrix of $\\mathbf{T}?$" :question/worked-solution "We can write $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v})$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(v) & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}24 \\\\ 16\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we have $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v}) = \\dfrac{1}{4}\\mathbf{v},$ we conclude that the linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ is a dilation with scale factor $k = \\dfrac{1}{4}.$\n\nRemember that the matrix representing a dilation of scale factor $k$ is given by\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052682/assets/4866597a50fbdb530e9275c77f2ca926c932c7cc334635bd05191aaddd510d75.png)\n\nTherefore, the standard matrix of $\\mathbf{T}$ is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052682/assets/962b9458714f9b7f6b17fe2259e140dfabba443d8ecb5b9e2328172ab3f8a1a2.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5451"] :question/problem "What linear transformation is represented by the matrix\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052682/assets/6191f617d2c2e89e07c1cc349a5926874f11ea78d046185ce990e566225a7c79.png)" :question/worked-solution "Consider the standard basis $\\{ \\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j} \\}$ of the Euclidean plane $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and the unit square spanned by the vectors, as depicted below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052682/assets/9e322e6ed1ddb8f1d094633e931cd179b7eb9d6f47acd7c7d0809f113df91b22.png)\n\nWe can find the images of $\\{ \\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j} \\}$ under $T,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{′} & =T(i)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=i \\\\ {j}^{′} & =T(j)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we obtain the following image:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052682/assets/b93bb91dae85a3e3b40a8d0ade3d4f420064aaa6926431fa7f664c977bb8fd59.png)\n\nTherefore, the matrix represents the reflection across the $x$-axis." :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
